  "query_preview": "..Today...A low pressure system currently located \nover western Arizona continues to drift northeastward while \nweakening. Enhanced moisture and large-scale lift still accompany \nthis system, and weak showers continue to fire through the early \nmorning as a result. Some patchy fog has also been observed as \nmid-upper level clouds begin to clear, and areas of fog will be \npossible through the remainder of the morning. Low level moisture \nlingers into the daytime today, and partly clearing skies will \nallow for modest destabilization and diurnal scattered shower and \nthunderstorm activity. A few stronger thunderstorms may be \npossible especially towards eastern Arizona where higher shear \nwill be found. Besides the potential for storms, expect steady \nwinds with slightly below normal temperatures.\n\nTuesday...Another low pressure system is visible in mid-level water \nvapor satellite imagery, currently located off the the coast of\nthe Northwest CONUS. This system is forecast to rapidly dive \nsouthward along the west coast on Tuesday. This will assist in \nlifting the lingering disturbance northeastward out of Arizona, \nand will phase with a strengthening ridge over the central CONUS \nto reinforce a pressure gradient bringing south-southwesterly flow\nto our region. There will be continued diurnal shower activity, \nthough a slight downtick is anticipated from Monday due to the \ndeparture of the weak disturbance. Also expect breezier south- \nsouthwest winds and near-normal temperatures, likely the warmest \nof the week.\n\nWednesday and Thursday...The strong low pressure system will begin \nto push inland and carry a deeper surge of moisture. PWAT values (a \nmeasure of atmospheric moisture) are forecast to range from 0.7-1.0 \ninches on Wednesday. This is very anomalous for this time of \nyear. High chances for showers and thunderstorms are expected as a\nresult, beginning Wednesday morning and continuing through \nThursday. Most areas are forecast to receive at least 0.5 inches \nof rain, but local regions across eastern AZ may see 1-3 inches or\nlocally higher, including the Eastern Mogollon Rim, White \nMountains, and the Navajo Nation. Ensemble guidance still does \nhave some spread in the trajectory and how progressive the trough \nwill be, so there is room for change in total accumulations. WPC \nhas introduced a Slight Risk of excessive rainfall for all of \nNorthern and Central Arizona on Wednesday and Thursday. A risk of \nboth Flooding and Flash Flooding will be in play through the \nperiod. Also of note is a potential developing severe threat. SPC \nhas introduced a Marginal (category 1 out of 5) risk of severe \nweather on Wednesday. High shear is likely as a strong 500mb jet \nstreak moves overhead, but there is considerable uncertainty in \navailable instability. For now there is at least a mentionable \nthreat of severe weather through the period. Besides the rain and \nthunderstorm threats, expect gusty daytime winds up to 30-40 mph \neach day. \n\nFriday and onward...Activity is expected to trend downward by Friday \ninto the weekend after the main trough axis pushes past Northern AZ. \nSome slight chances for showers may linger depending on a potential \nremnant cut-off low, but there is considerable uncertainty. Expect \nlighter winds and temperatures remaining below normal otherwise.",
